= Glossary =

This glossary is a '''navigation aid'''. It gives short orientation to recurring BITwiki terms and points to the canonical page where the concept or project contract is maintained. A glossary definition does not override the linked article or standard.

== A ==

; Agent
: A human, software, or artificial system that can act within a defined context. For technical treatment see [[Intelligent agent]]; contribution by an agent does not by itself establish authority or correctness.

== B ==

; BITCORE
: The cognition/orchestration plane around BITwiki and BIThub. It may help research, reason, transform, or coordinate work; it is not the canonical knowledge store. See [[BITwiki:Ecosystem]].

; BIThub
: The interaction/workflow plane around canonical BITwiki knowledge, implemented through discussion/collaboration surfaces rather than a competing semantic source of truth. See [[BITwiki:Ecosystem]].

; BITwiki
: The durable public knowledge plane and the project that maintains it. Canonical knowledge is represented through MediaWiki pages and supported semantic/runtime structures. See [[BITwiki:Organization]] and [[BITwiki:Constitution]].

; Book Matter
: The recognizable intellectual role of material within an article—such as overview, evidence, methods, limitations, interpretation, examples, or references. It is a content-role vocabulary, not an Entity type. See [[BITwiki:Book Matter]].

== C ==

; Claim validation
: The process of asking whether available evidence, methods, sources, alternatives, and uncertainties justify a claim at the stated scope. See [[BITwiki:Claim validation]].

; Concept
: The broad current Entity type for an independently addressable abstract subject. It is not a replacement for Domain, Category, or every historical V1 “Idea.” See [[BITwiki:Entity types]].

; Constitution
: BITwiki's canonical founding instrument governing current institutional commitments. Earlier Charters are historical sources, not parallel constitutions. See [[BITwiki:Constitution]] and [[BITwiki:Foundations]].

== D ==

; Domain
: A subject/field dimension used to orient knowledge. Domain is distinct from Entity type, Category, Portal, and ontology. See [[BITwiki:Ontology]] and [[BITwiki:Navigation]].

== E ==

; Entity type
: A primary modeling lens for what kind of knowledge object a canonical page represents. Types are useful and revisable, not claims that reality has only one possible classification. See [[BITwiki:Entity types]].

; Epistemic audit
: A structured review of claims, sources, provenance, uncertainty, representations, or the mechanisms that publish them. See [[BITwiki:Epistemic audit]].

; Epistemic humility
: The discipline of treating knowledge and models as revisable, bounded, and vulnerable to error. See [[BITwiki:Epistemics]] and [[BITwiki:Four foundations]].

; Epistemic status
: A contextual standing assigned to a claim or knowledge object when useful. Current values are Hypothetical, Emerging, Supported, Well-supported, Established, and Disputed. It is not a universal numeric confidence score. See [[BITwiki:Epistemic status]].

; Evidence
: Information or observation that bears on a claim under a particular inferential and methodological context. A source is not automatically evidence for every claim it contains. See [[BITwiki:Evidence]].

== F ==

; Four Foundations
: BITwiki's philosophical treatment of Ontology, Epistemology, Axiology, and Praxeology. See [[BITwiki:Four foundations]].

== H ==

; Holobiont
: A host organism considered together with its associated symbiotic microbial community. See [[Holobiont]].

; Holobiome
: A less standardized term for a broader host-associated or interconnected biological/microbial system; scope should be stated explicitly. See [[Holobiome]].

; Hyper-realism
: In the current Manifesto, the commitment to increasingly realistic, revisable models that preserve context and acknowledge their own construction without confusing a model with reality. See [[BITwiki:Manifesto]].

== I ==

; Implementation
: An Entity type for a concrete realization of a concept, method, protocol, or design where that modeling distinction is useful. See [[BITwiki:Entity types]].

; Interoperability
: Movement or mapping of knowledge between systems while preserving meaning, provenance, mapping direction, version context, and information loss. See [[BITwiki:Interoperability]].

== K ==

; Knowledge graph
: A view of explicitly represented entities, properties, and relationships. A graph layout or displayed edge is not independently a semantic fact. See [[BITwiki:Ontology]], [[BITwiki:Relationships]], and [[BITwiki:Navigation]].

; Knowledge lifecycle
: The movement from inquiry and source recovery through research, review, canonicalization, reuse, criticism, and revision. See [[BITwiki:Knowledge lifecycle]].

== M ==

; Manifesto
: BITwiki's statement of motivating conditions and aspirations, including Post-Truth, Post-Scarcity, Post-Intelligence, and Post-Fragmentation. See [[BITwiki:Manifesto]].

; MediaWiki
: The wiki platform used as BITwiki's publication substrate. Repository source, deployed MediaWiki state, and installed extension behavior are distinct evidence surfaces. See [[BITwiki:MediaWiki substrate]].

== O ==

; Ontology
: BITwiki's explicit model of kinds of knowledge objects and meaningful relationships. It is distinct from navigation and does not claim to exhaust reality. See [[BITwiki:Ontology]].

== P ==

; Portal
: A reader-facing entry point into a field or coherent knowledge surface. Portal hierarchy is navigation, not ontology inheritance. See [[BITwiki:Navigation]].

; Project
: An Entity type for an organized effort with goals, participants, or deliverables when the corpus needs to model the effort itself. See [[BITwiki:Entity types]].

; Property
: A Semantic MediaWiki attribute or relationship with a documented semantic job. New properties should emerge from repeated use/query needs rather than from historical catalogs. See [[BITwiki:Semantic properties]].

; Provenance
: Information about origin, attribution, transmission, transformation, review, and lineage needed to understand where a knowledge artifact or claim came from. See [[BITwiki:Provenance]].

== R ==

; Relationship
: An explicitly typed connection between modeled objects, with direction and meaning that must be justified independently of graph display. See [[BITwiki:Relationships]].

; Recursion
: Reapplication of a process or structure to its own outputs or to structures of the same kind. BITwiki uses recursion where it improves review, reuse, or self-correction; self-reference is not evidence of truth. See [[BITwiki:Knowledge lifecycle]] and [[BITwiki:Human-AI symbiosis]].

== S ==

; Semantic MediaWiki (SMW)
: The MediaWiki extension used for structured properties and queries. Its presence does not make every historical V1 property or inference rule current. See [[BITwiki:MediaWiki substrate]] and [[BITwiki:Semantic properties]].

; Source
: A record, publication, dataset, testimony, artifact, or other origin from which information may be obtained. Source role and reliability depend on the question. See [[BITwiki:Sources]].

; Stewardship
: Identifiable responsibility for maintaining or deciding within a defined project scope. Authority remains bounded, reviewable, and answerable to reasons. See [[BITwiki:Constitution]] and [[BITwiki:Editorial protocol]].

== T ==

; Template
: A reusable MediaWiki authoring/rendering interface. Templates may emit semantic state when explicitly designed to do so, but they are not an independent semantic authority. See [[BITwiki:Templates and categories]].

; Transclusion
: Inclusion of one maintained MediaWiki source in another page. It is a composition mechanism, not a proof mechanism, retrieval API, or guarantee that source annotations retain the same subject in the host. See [[BITwiki:Transclusion]] and [[Help:Transclusion]].

== U ==

; Uncertainty
: A limitation in knowledge, measurement, model structure, concepts, language, stochastic processes, or other parts of an inference. See [[BITwiki:Uncertainty]].

== V ==

; Validation
: Testing whether a claim, representation, schema, template, module, or other artifact satisfies the standards relevant to that object. Validation of source structure or software does not certify factual truth. See [[BITwiki:Claim validation]], [[BITwiki:Epistemic audit]], and [[BITwiki:Lua architecture]].

